      <h2>The real price of chasing GEO when it is not your bottleneck</h2>
      <p>
        A mid-market SaaS company spending £2,000 monthly on a GEO retainer is paying roughly £24,000 annually for the privilege of watching a metric oscillate. Research published by Kevin Indig shows only 2.37% of pages cited in ChatGPT also appear in Perplexity, meaning "AI visibility" across engines is nearly uncorrelated. That same £24,000 spent on analyst relations (analyst inclusion costs roughly £3,000-5,000 per briefing, so four briefings annually) would place the company in four industry reports that AI systems license for training. The return on analyst spend is visible and auditable: you can check whether you appear in the new Gartner report. The return on GEO retainers is a dashboard number that moves by ±20 percentage points monthly based on model version updates alone, not anything your team did. A founder asking "should we hire a GEO consultant?" should first ask "are we in Legal500, Chambers, or the analyst databases our customers consult?" If the answer is no, GEO is misdirected. The visibility block is editorial, not technical. Fixing it requires publication placement and analyst relations, which are PR problems, not GEO problems.
      </p>

      <h2>Why GEO tool churn peaked in 2025, and why it stays high</h2>
      <p>
        Most companies that trial GEO tools abandon them by month six. The reason is not that the tools are inaccurate; it is that accuracy confirms what the company should have already suspected: the tool is measuring something close to random noise. A firm that pays £500 monthly for a tracker and sees its citation share jump from 8% to 15% over three months then drop to 10% the next month is learning that the number moves on its own. That pattern plays out identically across every entry-level and mid-market tool, because the underlying measurement problem is not tool-specific. Citation volatility across AI systems is structural, not a tooling immaturity that better software can solve. The firms that do retain tools past month six are either: (1) large enough that a dashboard serves a reporting need to the board regardless of measurement accuracy, or (2) small enough that they bought the cheapest entry-tier tool and forgot to cancel it.
      </p>

      <h2>Identifying an overpriced GEO proposal: the five price-signal red flags</h2>
      <p>
        When a vendor or agency pitches a GEO engagement, five pricing signals indicate you are being quoted an inflated rate. First: lack of transparency about what components cost. A proposal that says "£2,000/month for comprehensive GEO strategy" without itemising tool cost, labour cost, and underlying visibility work cost is hiding the margins. Demand a breakdown showing exactly which £2,000 covers which activities. Legitimate vendors can do this; consultants unwilling to do it are either unclear on their own cost structure or inflating the rate to absorb your future complaints. Second: long minimum contracts without performance benchmarks. A vendor asking for a 12-month contract upfront without defining what "success" means (referral traffic targets, not dashboard movement) is taking on zero risk while you take all of it. Standard contracts in adjacent fields (PR, analyst relations) run 3-month minimums with month-to-month after. A GEO vendor demanding 12 months upfront is betting on your inertia, not on their results. Third: coupling tool access to retainer pricing. A vendor charging £2,000 monthly that includes Nightwatch (list price £299) is absorbing the Nightwatch cost into their margin. If you can buy Nightwatch directly at £299 and hire a freelance strategist at £100/hour, the agency retainer is likely pricing out higher than its components are worth. Fourth: refusing to name which specific tool they use. A vendor saying "our proprietary AI visibility measurement" without explaining what they are actually querying, how often they rescan, or what model versions they test is signalling that their secret sauce is not reproducible. Secret sauce in measurement is a red flag; reproducible methodologies are a green flag. Fifth: promises of outcome guarantees tied to their own scoring. A vendor promising "we guarantee 25% citation share improvement" measured by their own tool is making a promise about their measurement tool, not about your actual visibility. Referral traffic is the only outcome you can guarantee; citation share movement by their own metrics is not a guarantee. These five red flags together suggest a proposal that prioritises the vendor's revenue over your outcome. The best proposals break these signals: transparent pricing, short-term optionality, named tools at their published rate, reproducible methodology, and outcomes measured by referral traffic you can audit yourself.
      </p>

      <h2>The specific cost to a bootstrapped firm of not knowing GEO is risky</h2>
      <p>
        A founder with £3,000 monthly revenue and £500 monthly to allocate to marketing faces a choice that mid-market firms do not. Every pound matters. If the founder spends £200 on a GEO retainer and £300 on tools, they have allocated 40 per cent of discretionary marketing budget to a channel whose effect is unmeasurable. If they spend £500 on publication placement or analyst relations instead, the effect is immediately auditable: the founder can verify the article was published, check how many publications picked it up, measure referral traffic. The cost of being wrong about GEO increases with company stage inversely. A founder with £500 monthly budget being wrong about GEO costs everything. A mid-market firm with £20,000 monthly budget being wrong about GEO costs 10 per cent—material but survivable. This is why bootstrapped businesses are the primary victims of GEO marketing: they have no margin for error and no in-house expertise to call baloney. They trust a consultant, spend six months and three to five thousand pounds, see no referral traffic movement, and conclude "AI visibility is not for us" when the real conclusion should be "I allocated my budget to the wrong channel." The data on this: firms that abandoned GEO after six months cited not inaccurate tools but failure to move referral traffic. None of them needed a better tool; they needed to reallocate that budget to the visibility work (publication placement, analyst relations, community participation) that creates citations in the first place.
      </p>

      <h2>How to audit a GEO claim without a tool</h2>
      <p>
        When a vendor or consultant pitches GEO, ask three questions and demand verifiable answers. First: "If I block GPTBot and ClaudeBot in my robots.txt today, will I stop appearing in ChatGPT within 90 days?" The honest answer is "No, because ChatGPT will use its search crawler (OAI-SearchBot), which is different, and because the model was trained before your block." If a consultant says yes, they do not understand how AI retrieval works. Second: "Show me three case studies from clients with similar industry, size and budget to mine, where you moved their referral traffic from zero to measurable." Demand that the studies include referrer logs, not tool dashboards. Most consultants cannot produce these because they do not exist. Third: "What is your definition of success, and how will we measure it?" If the answer is "we will move your tool dashboard up 15 per cent," you have your answer: success is defined in a noisy metric. If the answer is "we will move your ChatGPT referrer traffic from zero to twenty per week within four months," that is measurable and honest. Ask for a written contract with that benchmark and a 90-day escape clause. Few consultants will agree, because most cannot deliver on referrer-traffic promises.
      </p>

